The contract tests are all working fine in visual studio but I am not able to generate the Pex method tests for the same. I've added the [PexMethod] attribute to the methods and the [PexClass] attribute to the class. The code gets compiled but no tests are generated.

A: The issue comes from the way Pex works. Pex generates input by trying every possible outcome. In your case, "PUT_AddContract" method is trying to add the value x to the ArrayList arrList, so it has every possible int value as a possible input. 

It seems that the array list can only contain 10 elements maximally. You can avoid testing all integers by introducing a second ArrayList without limit and use this one only for the test:

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrListNoLimit, [PexNoTest]([PexAssumeUnderTest]) ArrayList arrList, int x)
{ /* similar to your current implementation */  }

In conclusion, Pex generates tests with int value in the range of -536870912 to 536870911 with a frequency of 16704 (and it starts from 0). It seems that your ArrayList has a limit of 10 elements. So, the test cases generated by Pex are just valid for the existing elements in your ArrayList. If you add 7th, 8th, 9th and 10th int values to your ArrayList (the limit), it is going out of the frequency range and you should see the error "The maximum frequency count was exceeded". If you don't mind always adding same elements, you can try to increase the frequency which can be added to the PexSettings.
Hope it helped to understand the Pex's behavior.

A: The problem here comes from how Pex can generate tests for a method that manipulates the count property of a collection.  It's not practical to have a test case in which Pex tries to insert 11th element into an ArrayList with a count of 10.  Each case represents a distinct set of elements in the list, so when the list is full, the Pex has to make a decision on which elements to keep, which ones to replace, and which ones to ignore.  
So, in order to have Pex generate a test case for the 11th element, you would have to increase the capacity of the ArrayList, which is not allowed according to the documentation of ArrayList.
I suspect if you create a new ArrayList and assign the content of the original one into the new one, it should work as long as you keep adding to the new one.
Another workaround is to create your method such that it only inserts elements if the current count is less than 10, then--------------------            int New_Ret2 = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_Ret2_IsNegative = (New_Ret2 < 0);
            bool New_arrListContainsX2 = arrList.Contains(x);
            Assert.IsTrue( (New_Ret2 < 0) == (!New_arrListContainsX2));


        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListAfterRemoveAtx = (int)(arrList[index]);

            arrList.RemoveAt(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_arrListcontainsAfterRemoveAtx = arrList.Contains(Old_arrListAfterRemoveAtx);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_AfterRemoveAtx", Old_arrListAfterRemoveAtx);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_AfterRemoveAtx", New_arrListcontainsAfterRemoveAtx);

        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
